Absolutely—here’s a quick “keep doing this” list. These are solid choices you should preserve as you harden the stack:

Architecture & flow

Clear separation of concerns: ClientMessage/ServerMessage vs. UI‑facing NetworkMessage keeps network I/O decoupled from app logic.

Two‑channel model: control on reliable, voice on a dedicated unreliable channel—exactly right for real‑time audio.

Serialize once, fan‑out (server): you already build one encoded buffer and reuse it for broadcast—great for CPU/cache.

broadcast_message_except for voice: avoids self‑echo without extra client‑side checks.

Per‑tick update loop with Instant deltas on both client and server: simple, predictable pacing.

Configurability & operability

YAML config on the server (host/port, names, channel IDs, buffer sizes): makes ops changes safe and reviewable.

Channel memory caps (max_memory_usage_bytes) for voice: good guardrail against floods.

Periodic stats with bytes in/out and counts: gives you a baseline for regression and capacity tracking.

UX & lifecycle

Init handshake (InitClient with full roster + greeting): lets clients render the UI consistently after connect.

User presence events (ClientConnected / ClientDisconnected): clean lifecycle signaling.

Queueing to message_queue on the client + poll_messages(): keeps UI reactive without blocking network code.

Disconnect reason propagation: surfacing why we dropped helps users and debugging.

Data & serialization

Serde + bincode for compact payloads: right trade‑off for game/voice control paths.

Username in netcode user_data: lightweight identity handoff at auth time—keeps the protocol minimal.

Logging & safety

Minimal, informative logs (✅/❌/💬 tags) around major events: readable in production.

Server‑side checks (warn if voice hits reliable): early detection of client mis‑routes.

Netcode/transport choices

renet + renet_netcode: leverages a battle‑tested transport with connection management instead of rolling your own.

Socket bound to 0.0.0.0:0 on the client: OS‑assigned ephemeral port—good default for NAT friendliness.

If you keep these patterns and layer the hardening we listed earlier (seq/timestamps for voice, bounded bincode, drift‑aware jitter, RT‑safe callbacks on audio, etc.), you’ll have a production‑grade, low‑latency stack that behaves well on real networks and a wide variety of devices.
